Hollow Multi-Shelled Structure with Metal Organic Frameworks Evolutive Heterogeneous Casing for Enhanced Lithium Storage
Benefiting from the high theoretical capacity, low cost and wide availability, \(\mathsf{S n O}_{2}\) -based materials have attracted great research interest as high-performance anode materials for lithium-ion batteries (LIBs).[11] However, \(\mathsf{S n O}_{2}\) anode materials still suffer from poor capacity retention over extended charge-discharge cycling due to the large volume inflation \((>\!300\%)\) caused by the alloying reaction with Li to form \(\mathsf{L i}_{4.4}\mathsf{S n}\).[2] To solve this problem, three ways have been proposed: 1) Building buffer structure of \(\mathsf{S n O}_{2}\) -based electrode materials with the hollow or porous nanostructures,[3] to buffer the stress of the volume expansion. Among them, hollow multi-shelled structure (HoMS), which has been abbreviated as HoMS by Wang's group,[4] shows better effect; 2) Coating physical buffer layer with the carbon or silica on the surface of \(\mathsf{S n O}_{2}\) can protect it from collapse during long term reactions that will greatly
enhance the cycling stability[5] and reversible capacity[6]; 3) Coating physical and chemical buffer layer with heterogeneous metal oxide on the surface of \(\mathsf{S n O}_{2}\) -based electrode material, such as \(\mathsf{S n O}_{2}@\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}\) and \(\mathsf{S n O}_{2}@\mathsf{C o}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}\),[7] can greatly enhance the structure stability and improve the storage capacity simultaneously. However, there is no general approach to achieve the above three structural designs at the same time.
HoMSs have attracted a lot of attention for their excellent performance in energy density and capacity of lithium-ion batteries (LIBs).[8] Compared with the conventional solid electrode structures, HoMSs are the better buffer structures to improve the structure stability of electrodes, because the hollow interior space within HoMSs can well buffer the stress of the volume expansion caused by \(\mathsf{L i}^{+}\) -insertion/extraction process.[9] The coating approach is reported as the widely usable and efficient method in modifying electrode performance for LIBs by different coating materials.[10] Especially, metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) can easily combine with other materials to construct heterogeneous structures, which makes MOFs an outstanding coating material in catalysis, sensors, and gas storage and separation.[11] Additionally, as the highly ordered periodic distribution of metal nodes and organic ligands within MOFs, their evolved porous carbons or metal oxides will possess genetic structural information of MOFs and demonstrate positive effect on promoting material performance in electrochemical catalysis and energy storages.[12]
Herein, we introduce a general approach to design heterogeneous HoMSs by using MOFs evolutive casing. Adopted as electrode materials in LIBs, these heterogeneous HoMSs possess the following advantages: 1) HoMSs are good buffer structures to well buffer the stress of the volume expansion over extended charge-discharge cycling in LIBs; 2) the MOFs evolutive casing can be precisely designed as not only the protective carbon layer (physical buffer layer) but also the heterogeneous metal oxide layer (physical and chemical buffer layer), which can help to promote the cyclic stability and energy capacity of electrodes for LIBs. Additionally, by utilizing different HoMSs and MOFs, we can design various heterogeneous HoMSs by this method.
As shown in route 1 of Scheme 1, HoMS of triple-shelled \(\mathsf{S n O}_{2}\) (TS-SnO<sub>2</sub>) can be synthesized by our previous method.[13] Then, a MIL-100 (Fe) layer was properly coated on the TS-SnO<sub>2</sub> to construct the TS-SnO<sub>2</sub>@MIL-100 (Fe) (synthetic details can be found in supporting information).[14] Afterwards, the TS-SnO<sub>2</sub>@FeO<sub>x</sub>-C (MOF) was collected after calcination in nitrogen atmosphere. Further evolutive heterogeneous structures of TS-SnO<sub>2</sub>@FeO<sub>3</sub> (MOF) can be obtained through a second calcination in air condition with TS-SnO<sub>2</sub>@FeO<sub>x</sub>-C (MOF). As anode materials in LIBs, TS-SnO<sub>2</sub>@FeO<sub>3</sub> (MOF) exhibits good storage capacity and cycling performance, which can be attributed to the MOFs evolutive casing: 1) the outer evolutive Fe<sub>2</sub>O<sub>3</sub> casing can well protect the inner SnO<sub>2</sub>; 2) MOFs evolutive
