\(\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}\) casing will possess large surface area and provide more lithium-storage sites to maintain the high storage capacity.
As shown in Figure 1a, 1b and Figure S1, uniform \(\mathsf{T S-S n O}_{2}\) HoMSs possess an average diameter of about \(1\ \upmu\mathrm{m}\) and an interlayer distance of \(0.335\,\mathsf{n m}\) which can be ascribed to the (110) facet of tetragonal crystal system. After MOFs coating process, a fully and uniformly coated MIL-100 (Fe) layer can be obviously observed on the surface of \(\mathsf{T S-S n O}_{2}\) HoMSs with measured thickness of about \(50~\mathsf{n m}\) (Figure 1c). Both \(\mathsf{T S-S n O}_{2}@\mathsf{F e O}_{x}\mathrm{-C}\) (MOF) (Figure 1d) and \(\mathsf{T S-S n O}_{2}@\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}\) (MOF) (Figure 1e) display triple-shelled hollow structures with the full and uniform evolved casings, which demonstrate excellent structural stability of hybrid HoMSs even after two-step calcination process. Spherical aberration-corrected high-resolution transmission electron microscopy (ACTEM) image further reveals (012) facet of \(\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}\) nanoparticles (Figure 1f). The elemental mapping images (Figure S2) demonstrate the uniform distribution of Fe and Sn elements over the outer and inner surface within TS\(\mathsf{S n O}_{2}@\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}\) (MOF). The geometric details of different samples were also investigated by field-emission scanning electron microscope (FESEM) images (Figure S3a-3c) which demonstrate
the triple-shelled hollow structures over the entire preparation and evolution process. The Sn:Fe molar ratio was determined to be 20:1 by energy dispersive X-ray (EDX) spectroscopy (Figure S3d and Table S1) and ICP analysis.
Powder X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns indicate that all the main peaks can be indexed to rutile \(\mathsf{S n O}_{2}\) (JCPDS no. 41-1445) (Figure 2a). Two weak peaks at \(10.44^{\circ}\) and \(18.71^{\circ}\) are assigned
respectively to the (428) and (9511) planes of MIL-100 (Fe) well matched with X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) results (Figure S4).[15] The small size of MIL-100(Fe) results in pronounced peak broadening. The formation of mixed iron oxide nanoparticles in \(\mathsf{T S-S n O}_{2}@\mathsf{F e O}_{x}\mathrm{-C}\) (MOF) is confirmed by the appearance of weak peaks at \(30.15^{\circ}\) , \(31.94^{\circ}\) , and \(44.03^{\circ}\) . Based on the diffraction peak at \(35.6^{\circ}\) of \(\mathsf{T S-S n O}_{2}@\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}\) (MOF), the coated layer was assigned to the rhombohedral \(\mathsf{a-F e}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}\) phase (JCPDS no. 33-0664)[16] which was further confirmed by ACTEM (Figure 1f). Thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) of \(\mathsf{T S-S n O}_{2}@\mathsf{M I L-}\) 100 (Fe) (Figure 2b) indicated three mass loss events: (1) removal of solvent molecules, (2) loss of guest molecules and degradation of small organics, and (3) ligand degradation and formation of final product \(\mathsf{a-F e}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}\) after \(360\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) . A low calcination temperature was chosen to remove carbon residue and transform \(\mathsf{F e O}_{x}\mathrm{-C}\) to \(\mathfrak{a}\) \(\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}\) and keep the genetic structural information of MOFs simultaneously. The Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR) spectra of \(\mathsf{T S-S n O}_{2}@\mathsf{M I L-100}\) (Fe) (Figure 2c) shows characteristic peaks corresponding to the carboxylate groups and aromatic C-H vibrations from the organic ligands, confirming successful coating of MIL-100(Fe) in the hybrid material.[17] The disappearance of these peaks in \(\mathsf{T S-S n O}_{2}@\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}\) (MOF) supports the full transformation of MIL-100 (Fe) to \(\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}\) Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) analysis demonstrates the type IV \(\mathsf{N}_{2}\) isotherm of these composite materials. The BET surface area of \(\mathsf{T S-S n O}_{2}@\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}\) (MOF) increased eightfold to \(319\,\mathsf{\ m}^{2}\,\mathsf{\ g}^{-1}\) compared to the pure \(\mathsf{T S-S n O}_{2}\) hollow structures (Figure 2d and
Table S2), which is attributed to the porous \(\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}\) structure evolved from MIL-100 (Fe) nanocrystals. In addition, the pore size distribution indicates the presence of hierarchical porosity in hybrid materials (Figure S5). The increased pore volume and hierarchical pore sizes in \(\mathsf{T S-S n O}_{2}@\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}\) (MOF) may promote electrolyte transport, as well as \(\mathsf{L i}^{+}\) ions insertion and extraction, thus improving the charge-discharge properties of these anode materials in LIBs.[18] In addition, the generality of the MOF-coating strategy was demonstrated by coating MIL-100 (Fe) on different multi-shelled metal oxides hollow structures, such as \(\mathsf{T i O}_{2}\), NiO and \(\mathsf{C o}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}\). As shown in Figure S6-S13, the successful formation of MIL-100 (Fe) casing on the representative HoMSs was confirmed by direct observation SEM and TEM images, XRD patterns, and FT-IR spectra. (The experiment details can be found in Supporting Information) This method will offer a new strategy to obtain a series of HoMS@MOFs with controllable crystalline and mass loading.
To investigate the electrochemical performance of above hybrid HoMSs in LIBs, the standard HoMS/Li half-cell configurations were assembled with different HoMSs as well as the \(\mathsf{T S-S n O}_{2}@\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}\) (particle)(Figure S14) fabricated by hydrothermal deposition (route 2 in Scheme 1) with less pore volume as a comparison (the details are described in the supporting information). The electrochemical reaction of \(\mathsf{L i}^{+}\) with \(\mathsf{S n O}_{2}\) and \(\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}\) can be described in Equation (1)-(5), respectively.[19]
\(\mathsf{S n O}_{2}+4\,\mathsf{L i}^{+}+4\mathsf{e}^{-}\to\mathsf{S n}+2\mathsf{L i}_{2}0\) \(\mathsf{S n}+\mathsf{L i}_{2}0\leftrightarrow\mathsf{S n}0+2\mathsf{L i}^{+}+2\mathsf{e}^{-}\) \(\mathsf{S n O}+\mathsf{L i}_{2}0\leftrightarrow\mathsf{S n O}_{2}+2\mathsf{L i}^{+}+2\mathsf{e}^{-}\) \(\mathsf{S n}+\mathsf{x}\mathsf{L}\mathsf{i}^{+}+\mathsf{x}\mathsf{e}^{-}\leftrightarrow\mathsf{L i}_{2}\mathsf{S n}\) \((0\leq x\leq4.4)\) \(\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}+6\mathsf{L i}^{+}+6\mathsf{e}^{-}\leftrightarrow2\mathsf{F e}+3\mathsf{L i}_{2}0\)
Figure 3a shows the cyclic voltammetry (CV) curves of TS\(\mathsf{S n O}_{2}@\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}\) (MOF) at a scanning rate of \(2\;\mathsf{m V}\;\mathsf{s}^{-1}\) in a voltage range of \(0.005{-}3.0\;\mathrm{~V~}\). The first cycle of the \(\mathsf{T S-S n O}_{2}@\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}\) (MOF) shows three pairs of redox peaks. The broad reduction peak at about \(1.26\,\mathrm{V}\) is attributed to the formation of intermediate SnO phase. The following sharp reduction peak at \(0.63\;\mathrm{V}\) is due to the reduction of SnO to metallic Sn accompanied by SEI film formation.[20] The peak at \(0.10\;\mathrm{~V~}\) is ascribed to the alloying process with Li forming \(\mathsf{L i}_{x}\mathsf{S n}\). Correspondingly, the oxidation peaks at \(0.59\,\mathrm{V}\), \(1.25\,\mathrm{V}\) and \(1.92\,\mathrm{V}\) are assigned to the de-alloying process, the oxidation of Sn to SnO intermediate, and further oxidation to \(\mathsf{S n O}_{2}\), respectively.[21] There is no obvious peak related to Fe-based species due to the low content of the \(\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}\) coating. In the subsequent cycle, the reduction peaks related to SnO formation and metallic Sn formation shift a little to \(1.21\,\mathrm{V}\) and \(0.80\;\mathrm{V}\), respectively, which should be caused by the irreversible SEI film. The peak of alloying process splits into two weaker peaks at \(0.32\;\mathrm{V}\) and \(0.09\;\mathrm{V}\), indicating that the alloying process evolves to two steps.[13] The corresponding oxidation peaks show obvious change in the second cycle. All the redox peaks are highly reversible in the following cycle. Although, the CV curves of the \(\mathsf{T S-S n O}_{2}\) hollow structures show similar redox peaks (Figure S15), the redox peaks related to the conversion between \(\mathsf{S n O}_{2}\) and SnO gradually disappear after three cycles, indicating the irreversible reaction. The CV results demonstrate that the MOF evolutive heterogeneous \(\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}\) on \(\mathsf{T S-S n O}_{2}@\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}\) (MOF) is beneficial to promote the reversibility between \(\mathsf{S n O}_{2}\) and \(\mathsf{S n O}\). To further study the charge transport and transfer characteristics
of the different electrodes, the alternating current impedance measurements were performed with \(\mathsf{T S-S n O}_{2}\)-HoMSs lithium \((Z_{\mathrm{w}})\), respectively.[5a] Typically, \(\mathsf{T S-S n O}_{2}@\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}\) (MOF) hollow structures have the smallest semicircle in the high frequency region among various electrodes (Figure 3b). The rich porous structure and higher BET surface area of \(\mathsf{T S-S n O}_{2}@\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}\) (MOF) are beneficial to \(\mathsf{L i}^{+}\) ions diffusion, thus decrease the ion transport resistance.[22] By contrast, the \(\mathsf{T S-S n O}_{2}@\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}\) (particle) with less pores and disordered iron oxide particles possess a larger semicircle in the high frequency region, which demonstrates a worse \(\mathsf{L i}^{+}\) ions diffusion ability.
The cycling performances of the electrodes were measured at a current density of \(100\;\mathsf{m A}\;\mathsf{g}^{-1}\) (Figure 3c and 3d). For the first cycle, the obtained higher specific discharge capacities of \(\mathsf{T S-S n O}_{2}\)-HoMSs, \(\mathsf{T S-S n O}_{2}@\mathsf{F e O}_{x}\)-C (MOF) and \(\mathsf{T S-S n O}_{2}@\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}\) (MOF) were 1304, 1700 and \(1908\;\mathsf{m A h}\;\mathsf{g}^{-1}\), respectively. The higher experimental capacity than theoretical capacity and the lower coulombic efficiencies (60.2%, 61.6% and 62.1%, respectively) in the first few cycles can be ascribed to decomposition of electrolyte and formation of solid electrolyte interface (SEI) film. As shown in Figure 3c, the \(\mathsf{T S-S n O}_{2}\)-HoMSs
