exhibit higher capacity and better cycling performance than \(\mathsf{S n O}_{2}\) electrodes with other morphologies which have been reported under similar test conditions.[23] Because HoMS can well buffer the volume expansion caused by Sn alloying with Li (Figure S16). However, in the pure \(\mathsf{T S-S n O}_{2}\) electrodes, the electrochemical reaction of Li with \(\mathsf{S n O}_{2}\) has an irreversible process along with the volume expansion. On this account, the cyclic capacity of pure \(\mathsf{T S-S n O}_{2}\) electrodes reduced more evidently than TS\(\mathsf{S n O}_{2}@\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}\) (MOF) hybrid materials after 70 cycles. Significantly, \(\mathsf{T S-S n O}_{2}@\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}\) (MOF) (theoretical capacity \(780\times87.5\%+1006\times12.5\%=808\ m A h\ g^{-1}\)) exhibited high specific capacity values and cyclic capacity retention, getting a stable capacity of about 750 mAh \(\mathsf{g}^{-1}\) after 100 cycles. The excellent cycling ability originates from the fully and uniformly coated casing evolved from MOFs which can not only maintain a good self stability but also protect the inner \(\mathsf{S n O}_{2}\) structure. Moreover, the reversible electrochemical reaction: \(\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}+6\mathsf{L i}^{+}+6\mathsf{e}^{-}\leftrightarrow2\mathsf{F e}+\) \(3\mathsf{L i}_{2}0\) could possibly occur in the hybrid materials TS\(\mathsf{S n O}_{2}@\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}\) (MOF) during the reduction/oxidation reaction of Li and \(\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}\) and Li-Sn alloying/de-alloying reaction, so the electrochemical performance improves obviously. By contrast, \(\mathsf{T S-S n O}_{2}@\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}\) (particle) exhibits poor cycling stability and the discharge capacity drop rapidly after 20 cycles because of uneven volume expansion caused by nonporous \(\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}\) nanoparticles. The retention capacity after 100 cycles for the evolutive \(\mathsf{S n O}_{2}@\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}\) (MOF)-HoMSs is about eight times higher than that of the \(\mathsf{S n O}_{2}@\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}\) (particles)-HoMSs.During the discharge cycle, the voltage plateau of electrodes was observed around \(0.8\ \mathrm{V}\), which were consistent with the reduction reaction of \(\mathsf{S n O}_{2}\) and \(\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}\). However, because of the relative low content of \(\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}\) in the hybrid materials, the voltage plateau of \(\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}\) to \(\mathsf{F e}^{0}\) and \(\mathsf{L i}_{2}\mathsf{O}\) could not be obviously observed in the Figure 3d. This also could be explained that the capacity of \(\mathsf{T S-S n O}_{2}@\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}\) (MOF) increased a little after coating the higher theoretical iron oxides porous nanoparticles. Additionally, TS\(\mathsf{S n O}_{2}@\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}\) (MOF) demonstrated a better coulombic efficiency than that of \(\mathsf{T S-S n O}_{2}@\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}\) (particle) (Figure S17). To verify the advanced nature of the two-step calcination process for TS\(\mathsf{S n O}_{2}@\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}\) (MOF), we prepare a contrastive sample of TS\(\mathsf{S n O}_{2}@\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}\)-air by directly heating \(\mathsf{T S-S n O}_{2}@\mathsf{M I L}100\) (Fe) in air condition, which demonstrated the much worse cycling performance (Figure S18) in comparison with \(\mathsf{T S-S n O}_{2}@\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}\) (MOF).The rate performance is also one of the most important factors to testify the high specific capacity and structure stability of the electrode materials.[24] As shown in Figure 3e, the discharge capacity of electrodes decreased at higher current densities, but the \(\mathsf{T S-S n O}_{2}@\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}\) (MOF) achieved better rate performance than pure \(\mathsf{T S-S n O}_{2}\)-HoMSs. Especially, at a current density of 2 A \(\mathsf{g}^{-1}\), the \(\mathsf{T S-S n O}_{2}@\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}\) (MOF) could maintain a capacity of 540 mAh \(\mathsf{g}^{-1}\), which was almost thrice higher than that of TS\(\mathsf{S n O}_{2}\)-HoMSs. The better rate performance could be attributed to the uniform MOF-evolved porous iron oxide casing on the shells of \(\mathsf{T S-S n O}_{2}\)-HoMSs that will facilitate rapid ion/charge transfer and maintain the stability of HoMS structure. In addition, 64% of the discharge capacity of \(\mathsf{T S-S n O}_{2}@\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}\) (MOF) was still retained when the current density back to \(100\ \mathsf{m A g}^{-1}\). This result is attributed to the porous and "breatable" features of MOF-evolved iron oxide casing on the surface of \(\mathsf{T S-S n O}_{2}\)-HoMSs
During the discharge cycle, the voltage plateau of electrodes was observed around \(0.8\ \mathrm{V}\) , which were consistent with the reduction reaction of \(\mathsf{S n O}_{2}\) and \(\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}\) . However, because of the relative low content of \(\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}\) in the hybrid materials, the voltage plateau of \(\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}\) to \(\mathsf{F e}^{0}\) and \(\mathsf{L i}_{2}\mathsf{O}\) could not be obviously observed in the Figure 3d. This also could be explained that the capacity of \(\mathsf{T S-S n O}_{2}@\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}\) (MOF) increased a little after coating the higher theoretical iron oxides porous nanoparticles. Additionally, TS\(\mathsf{S n O}_{2}@\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}\) (MOF) demonstrated a better coulombic efficiency than that of \(\mathsf{T S-S n O}_{2}@\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}\) (particle) (Figure S17). To verify the advanced nature of the two-step calcination process for TS\(\mathsf{S n O}_{2}@\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}\) (MOF), we prepare a contrastive sample of TS\(\mathsf{S n O}_{2}@\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}\)-air by directly heating \(\mathsf{T S-S n O}_{2}@\mathsf{M I L}100\) (Fe) in air condition, which demonstrated the much worse cycling performance (Figure S18) in comparison with \(\mathsf{T S-S n O}_{2}@\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}\) (MOF).
The rate performance is also one of the most important factors to testify the high specific capacity and structure stability of the electrode materials.[24] As shown in Figure 3e, the discharge capacity of electrodes decreased at higher current densities, but the \(\mathsf{T S-S n O}_{2}@\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}\) (MOF) achieved better rate performance than pure \(\mathsf{T S-S n O}_{2}\)-HoMSs. Especially, at a current density of 2 A \(\mathsf{g}^{-1}\), the \(\mathsf{T S-S n O}_{2}@\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}\) (MOF) could maintain a capacity of 540 mAh \(\mathsf{g}^{-1}\), which was almost thrice higher than that of TS\(\mathsf{S n O}_{2}\)-HoMSs. The better rate performance could be attributed to the uniform MOF-evolved porous iron oxide casing on the shells of \(\mathsf{T S-S n O}_{2}\)-HoMSs that will facilitate rapid ion/charge transfer and maintain the stability of HoMS structure. In addition, 64% of the discharge capacity of \(\mathsf{T S-S n O}_{2}@\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}\) (MOF) was still retained when the current density back to \(100\ \mathsf{m A g}^{-1}\). This result is attributed to the porous and "breatable" features of MOF-evolved iron oxide casing on the surface of \(\mathsf{T S-S n O}_{2}\)-HoMSs
which will not only facilitate rapid ion/charge transfer but also maintain a good stability of HoMSs during the Li' insertion/extraction in LIBs. The excellent cycling performance and remarkable structure stability demonstrated the HoMSs of \(\mathsf{T S-S n O}_{2}@\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}\) (MOF) are ideal candidates for the high performance LIBs.
